<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Zhou</h1><p class="st-meta">951 CE – 960 CE · capital Kaifeng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last of the Five Dynasties, whose able emperor Chai Rong began the reunification of China completed by the Song.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a>, a leading general of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-han">Later Han</a>, was forced into rebellion when Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chengyou">Liu Chengyou</a> plotted to kill him in 950. After the emperor's death he took the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 951 as founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wu-zhou">Zhou dynasty</a>, the first non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> ruler of the north for decades.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a> lightened taxes and punishments. His adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chai-rong">Chai Rong (Emperor Shizong)</a>, who succeeded in 954, defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-han">Northern Han</a> and Khitan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaoping">Gaoping</a>, reorganised the palace army, closed thousands of Buddhist monasteries and melted bronze images for coin. He took the lands between the Huai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a> in 958 and recovered two of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> in 959.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chai-rong">Chai Rong</a> died in 959, leaving a child emperor. In 960 the palace army commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Zhao Kuangyin</a> was proclaimed emperor by his troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenqiao-mutiny">Chenqiao</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, which built on Later Zhou's army and policies.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-zhou.jpg" alt="Later Zhou" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Zhou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>951 CE – 960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaifeng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guo Wei (Emperor Taizu), Chai Rong (Emperor Shizong), Chai Zongxun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhou (Guo Wei)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=956&amp;focus=later-zhou" aria-label="Later Zhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7581 918 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Zhou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7901 1174L7901 1174M7901 1174L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7901 1174h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7901 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kaifeng. <a href="/atlas?year=956&amp;focus=later-zhou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
